  "worked_solution": "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=y,{f}_{1}=2y,$ and ${f}_{0}=3y.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={e}^{-2x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{-2x}) & ={e}^{-2x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{-2x})+2({e}^{-2x})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x})+3{({e}^{-2x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})+2{e}^{-2x}\\cdot (-2{e}^{-2x})+3{e}^{-4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\cdot 4{e}^{-2x}-4{e}^{-4x}+3{e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =4{e}^{-4x}-4{e}^{-4x}+3{e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
